/** \page compositespage Cartesian conversions and composites


\tableofcontents

\b Rack can also create radar image composites, sometimes called mosaics. In weather services, composites are often preferred to single-radar products.
This section lists related commands and examples. A list of all the related command is \ref commands_cart.
Motion vectors are a separate topic, discussed in \ref motion-composites .

\section cartesian  Mapping data from polar to Cartesian coordinates

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "shell": true,
  "workdir": "env/default"
}
\~

A single-radar Cartesian image is created simply with \c --cCreate (abbr. \c -c ) as follows:
\code{.sh}
rack volume.h5 --cCreate -o cartesian.h5
\endcode
where  \c --cCreate is actually a shorthand of <tt>--cAdd --cExtract 'DATA,WEIGHT'</tt> :
\~conf{.sh}
{
  "append_args": "--encoding C -o out/cart-single-radar.png"
}
\~
\code{.sh}
rack volume.h5 --cAdd --cExtract DATA,WEIGHT -o cartesian.h5
\endcode

\image html  cart-single-radar.png  "A single-radar Cartesian image." 
\image latex cart-single-radar.png  "A single-radar Cartesian image."

If not explicitly issued, the first data in the input volume – \c /dataset1/data1 – is used. 
Conversion to Cartesian coordinates has several default settings, collected below as command line options.

| Option                 | Argument                   | Default, based on input     |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------- | --------------------------- | 
| \c --cProj             | EPSG code, proj string, or "AEQD" | \c "AEQD" (Azimuthal Equidistant centered at the radar) |
| \c --cBBox             | xLL,yLL,xUR,yUR            | Matched to the radar range  | 
| \c --cSize             | width,height               | \c "500,500"                | 
| \c --cMethod           | compositing method         | \c "MAXIMUM"                |
| \c --select            | path,quantity,...          | \c "path=dataset1/data1"    |


\section compositing  Compositing

In \b Rack , any single-radar Cartesian image is technically a "single-radar composite".
Internally, the composite is a separate memory array that supports \e continuous \e accumulation of data. 
That means that arbitrarily many input volumes can be added. For example, this command creates a dBZ composite of three radars:

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "shell": true,
  "workdir": "env/composite-fi"
}
\~

\code{.sh}
rack \
    volume-fikor.h5 --select quantity=DBZH --cAdd \
    volume-fikan.h5 --select quantity=DBZH --cAdd \
    volume-fivih.h5 --select quantity=DBZH --cAdd \
    --cExtract DATA,WEIGHT \
    -o out/composite-test.h5 
\endcode

The first \c --cAdd command initialises the composite with current geographical settings.
As they are not given in this example, the composite applies AEQD projection centered around the first input (\c fikor , Korpo radar).

\note For clarity, the applied \c --select command appears repeated in the above example. It would be sufficient to put it before \e the \e first \c --cAdd command
– the selector is internally "understood" and stored as a \e composite \e input \e selector for all the remaining inputs.

Instead of repeating arguments on the command line, it is handy to use \c --script to define a routine repeated for each input. (More details on \ref scriptspage .)
Likewise, if file paths are lengthy, their shared parts can be set with \c --inputPrefix and \c --outputPrefix . 
The previous command can be hence reformulated as follows, with an extended set of output files:
\~conf{.sh}
{
    "shell": true,
    "tests": {
        "out/composite-test.h5":  true,
        "out/composite-test.tif": [
            {"similarity": 0.95},
            {"filename": "reference/composite-test-AEQD.tif", "similarity": 0.95, "on_fail": "warn"}
        ],
        "out/composite-test.png": {"similarity": 0.95},
        "out/composite-test.txt": true
    }
}
\~

\~remark
	"on_fail": "error|warn|ignore"
\~


\code{.sh}
rack \
    --cProj 3067 \
    --script '--select quantity=DBZH --cAdd' \
    volume-fikor.h5 \
    volume-fikan.h5 \
    volume-fivih.h5 \
    --cExtract DATA,WEIGHT \
    --outputPrefix out/ \
    -o composite-test.h5 \
    -o composite-test.tif \
    --palette default -o composite-test.png \
    -o composite-test.txt
\endcode

\image html  composite-test.png  "Appending several radars in a Cartesian image." 
\image latex composite-test.png  "Appending several radars in a Cartesian image." 



\~remark
stop
\~


\~remark 
rack \
    --cSize '640,768' \
    --cProj '3067' \
    --cBBox '-208000.0,6390000.0,1072000.0,7926000.0' \
    --script '--select quantity=DBZH --cAdd' \
    data/*.h5 \
    --cExtract DATA \
    -o composite-fi.tif

EPSG:3035 — LAEA Europe. Equal-area, official EEA/Eurostat standard
for European analysis. Reasonable distortion across all of
Finland. Best choice for areal statistics and environmental data.

EPSG:3034 — LCC Europe. Lambert Conformal Conic
(shape-preserving). Used for pan-European aeronautical
charting. Better than LAEA if angular fidelity matters more than area.

EPSG:4258 — ETRS89 geographic. Not a projected CRS (it's lat/lon), but
it is the European datum that all Finnish official projections are
built on. Good for data exchange.

Critical gap in the list: the actual Finnish national projection
EPSG:3067 (ETRS89 / TM35FIN, central meridian 27°E) is absent. That is
what Finnish authorities (NLS, FMI, SYKE) use and what rack presumably
targets. EPSG:25833 (UTM zone 33, central meridian 15°E) is 12° west
of Finland's center — it works but accumulates distortion. EPSG:3067
should probably be added explicitly to whatever server config or PROJ
list rack.maps maintains.

\~ 



An example of a command that generates a composite image of Pseudo CAPPI product of 16 radars:  

\~conf{.sh}
{
  "shell": true
}
\~


\include composite-fi_rack.sh
\linkfile{composite-fi_rack.sh}

\image html  composite-fi.svg.png  "A radar composite image – Finnish (FMI) radars, Pseudo CAPPI 600m, weighted-average compositing method: main product (left) and quality field (right)." 
\image latex composite-fi.svg.png  "A radar composite image – Finnish (FMI) radars, Pseudo CAPPI 600m, weighted-average compositing method: main product (left) and quality field (right)."   width=\textwidth


\b Experimental. The above command line can be generated (and executed)
using Python utility \c rack.composite .
The equivalent command line is not remarkably shorter, but now the order of the options is insignificant:

\include composite-fi_py.sh

\~conf{.py}
{
  "workdir": "env/composite-fi/",
  "imports": ["logging", "rack.prog","rack.composite"],
  "prepend_code": ["logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)"],
  "append_code": [
	"# rack_cmd = prog.to_string(fmt)"
   ],
  "tests" : {
  	"rack.sh": {
  	  "returncode": 0
  	}
  }
}
\~ 

\note In above examples, the input files can be given equivalently using shell wildcards, with a single plain argument like: \c data/20*.fi*.h5 . That also makes option \c --inputPrefix or \c --INDIR (for Python) unneeded.

The above command lines can be generated also inside a Python program, as follows.

\code{.py}
 
# Initialize command line generator.
composer = rack.cmdline.Composer(rack.composite)

# Set parameters
# To see thet supported command keywords, issue: python3 -m rack.composite

composer.set(PROJ=3067)
composer.set(BBOX=[-208000,6390000,1072000,7926000])
composer.set(SIZE=[640,768])
composer.set(METHOD='WAVG,1,8')

composer.set(INDIR='data/') 
composer.set(INFILE='202609261800_radar.polar.fi???.h5') 

composer.set(PPROD='pCappi,500')
# Alternatively, the measured parameter can be composited simply by selecting it: 
# composer.set(select='quantity=DBZH')

composer.set(OUTDIR='out')
composer.set(OUTFILE='composite-fi.h5')
composer.set(FORMATS='svg')
composer.set(svgRelativePaths=True)
composer.set(map='out/map-composite-fi.png')

composer.set(svgStyle='.IMAGE_BORDER=stroke:gray;width:1px')


# Extract command sequence
prog = composer.get_prog()

# Show generated command line: 
fmt = rack.cmdline.RackFormatter(params_format="'{params}'") 
print(prog.to_string(fmt))

# ... or execute directly:
# rack.process.run(prog, logger=logger)
\endcode


\~stop
\~

See also
- \subpage compositing_parameters_page
- \subpage compositing_schemes_page

\~exec
OUTFILE=composite-baltic.h5 DEMO=1 BBOX=0,47,40,73 SIZE=600,800 PRODUCT=pCappi,700 METHOD=WAVG,2,3,-32  ./make-composite.sh data-20140924  #exec 
composite -compose Over composite-baltic-rgba.png map-baltic-0,47,40,73-600,800.png composite-baltic-rgba-map.tif   #exec        
convert composite-baltic.png -type TrueColor tmp.tif
convert -frame 2  +append tmp.tif  composite-baltic-rgba-map.tif -resize 900x600  composite-baltic-pair.png
\~


*/


